  "slug": "optical-assisted-electro-2157",
  "summary": "Three November 2026 preprints report advances in silicon, fiber, and organic-crystal nonlinear optical sources.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of nonlinear optical and electro-optic interactions to generate and characterize coherent light. The first preprint demonstrates telecom-band optical parametric oscillation in a passive silicon microring at 4.5 K, pumped by a single continuous-wave source, with a reported 2.25 mW onset. The second demonstrates dual-comb spectroscopy using two gain-assisted fiber ring resonators with intracavity electro-optic modulation, yielding a 1.8 THz flat-top comb with tunable operation. The third uses the organic nonlinear crystal MBI, pumped by a 1.5 μm MHz fiber laser, for optical rectification and Pockels-effect THz generation and detection spanning 1 to 30 THz with reported performance above 40 dB. What is new across the set is the demonstration of these processes in specific platforms: cryogenic passive silicon, gain-assisted electro-optic fiber cavities, and an organic crystal.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-18"
}
